# The Ultimate Mechanical Engineering Terminology List

Here’s a comprehensive mechanical engineering vocabulary guide for students, covering the fundamentals you should know in college, in the lab, during internships, and in industry. It includes core concepts, equations-related terminology, manufacturing, materials, thermodynamics, fluids, machine design, CAD, controls, and professional engineering terms.

The goal isn't just to memorize definitions. You should eventually be able to **define each term, explain how it works, recognize it in a real system, and know when to use it.**

## 1\. Core engineering fundamentals

- Mechanics — Study of forces and their effects on bodies.
- Statics — Study of bodies in equilibrium.
- Dynamics — Study of bodies in motion.
- Kinematics — Description of motion without considering its causes.
- Kinetics — Study of motion in relation to forces and mass.
- Force — A push or pull that can change an object's motion.
- Moment — Turning effect of a force about a point or axis.
- Torque — Twisting moment, commonly associated with shafts and motors.
- Couple — Two equal and opposite forces separated by a distance that produce a pure moment.
- Equilibrium — State in which the net force and net moment are zero.
- Free-body diagram (FBD) — Diagram showing external forces and moments acting on an isolated body.
- Load — Force, moment, pressure, or other demand applied to a component.
- Reaction force — Force exerted by a support or constraint.
- Displacement — Change in position.
- Distance — Total length traveled along a path.
- Velocity — Rate of change of position.
- Speed — Magnitude of velocity.
- Acceleration — Rate of change of velocity.
- Angular displacement — Change in rotational position.


- **Angular velocity** — Rate of change of angular position.
- **Angular acceleration** — Rate of change of angular velocity.
- **Mass** — Measure of inertia.
- **Weight** — Gravitational force acting on a mass.
- **Inertia** — Resistance to changes in motion.
- **Center of mass** — Mass-weighted average position of a body.
- **Centroid** — Geometric center of an area or volume.
- **Moment of inertia** — A term for resistance to rotation or bending, depending on the type of inertia being discussed.
- **Second moment of area** — Geometric property governing resistance to bending.
- **Radius of gyration** — Equivalent distance that represents how mass or area is distributed relative to an axis.
- **Work** — Energy transferred by a force acting through a displacement.
- **Energy** — Capacity to do work.
- **Power** — Rate of energy transfer or work.
- **Efficiency** — Ratio of useful output to total input.
- **Impulse** — Force integrated over a time interval.
- **Momentum** — Mass multiplied by velocity.
- **Angular momentum** — Rotational counterpart of linear momentum.
- **Friction** — Resistance to relative motion between contacting surfaces.
- **Coefficient of friction** — Ratio relating friction force to normal force under a specified friction model.
- **Normal force** — Contact force perpendicular to a surface.
- **Tension** — Pulling force transmitted through a member or cable.
- **Compression** — Loading that tends to shorten a member.
- **Shear force** — Force acting parallel to a cross-section.
- **Bending moment** — Internal moment that bends a structural member.
- **Distributed load** — Load spread over a length, area, or volume.
- **Point load** — Load idealized as acting at one location.
- **Factor of safety (FoS)** — Design margin expressed as a ratio between a capacity and a demand, using the applicable design definitions.
- **Boundary condition** — Constraint or prescribed condition defining a problem's solution.
- **Initial condition** — State of a system at the beginning of an analysis.

## 2\. Mathematics and engineering analysis

- **Scalar** — Quantity with magnitude only.
- **Vector** — Quantity with magnitude and direction.
- **Component** — Part of a vector along a chosen axis.
- **Coordinate system** — Reference framework used to specify position and direction.
- **Cartesian coordinates** — Coordinates based on perpendicular axes.
- **Polar coordinates** — Coordinates based on radial distance and angle in a plane.
- **Cylindrical coordinates** — Radial, angular, and axial coordinates.
- **Spherical coordinates** — Coordinates based on distance and two angles.
- **Derivative** — Instantaneous rate of change.
- **Integral** — Mathematical operation used to accumulate quantities.
- **Differential equation** — Equation involving derivatives of unknown functions.
- **Ordinary differential equation (ODE)** — Differential equation with one independent variable.
- **Partial differential equation (PDE)** — Differential equation with multiple independent variables.
- **Linearization** — Approximation of a nonlinear model near a specified operating point.
- **Nonlinear system** — System whose governing relationships are not linear.
- **Eigenvalue** — Scalar associated with a particular eigenvector in a linear transformation.
- **Eigenvector** — Nonzero vector whose direction remains unchanged under a linear transformation.
- **Matrix** — Rectangular array of mathematical values.
- **Numerical methods** — Computational techniques for approximating mathematical solutions.
- **Interpolation** — Estimation between known data points.
- **Extrapolation** — Estimation beyond known data.
- **Regression** — Method for fitting a model to observed data.
- **Residual** — Difference between an observed value and a model prediction.
- **Convergence** — Tendency of an iterative solution to approach a stable result.
- **Error tolerance** — Accepted limit on numerical or measurement error.
- **Sensitivity analysis** — Study of how outputs change when inputs vary.
- **Dimensional analysis** — Analysis using physical dimensions to check relationships and develop models.
- **Dimensional homogeneity** — Requirement that both sides of a physical equation have consistent dimensions.
- **Similarity** — Correspondence between systems based on relevant geometric and physical scaling.
- **Nondimensionalization** — Rewriting equations in terms of dimensionless variables.
- **Order of magnitude** — Approximate scale of a quantity, usually by powers of ten.
- **Significant figures** — Digits representing the precision of a numerical value.
- **Uncertainty** — Quantified doubt associated with a measurement or result.
- **Propagation of uncertainty** — How input uncertainties affect a calculated output.
- **Curve fitting** — Finding a mathematical function that represents data.
- **Optimization** — Finding the best solution under a defined objective and constraints.

## 3\. Units, dimensions, and measurement

- **SI units** — International System of Units.
- **Imperial units** — Traditional unit system used in some engineering contexts.
- **US customary units** — Common US system including inches, feet, pounds, and Fahrenheit.
- **Base quantity** — Fundamental physical quantity such as length, mass, or time.
- **Derived quantity** — Quantity defined using base quantities.
- **Unit conversion** — Converting a value between compatible units.
- **Dimensional consistency** — Agreement of physical dimensions in an equation.
- **Resolution** — Smallest change an instrument can display or distinguish.
- **Accuracy** — Closeness of a result to an accepted reference value.
- **Precision** — Closeness of repeated results to one another.
- **Repeatability** — Agreement under repeated measurements using the same conditions.
- **Reproducibility** — Agreement when measurement conditions vary.
- **Calibration** — Comparing an instrument with a reference to establish its measurement behavior.
- **Traceability** — Connection of a measurement result to recognized references through a documented calibration chain.
- **Systematic error** — Error that consistently shifts measurements in a particular way.
- **Random error** — Error that varies unpredictably between measurements.
- **Significant digits** — Digits retained to represent numerical precision.
- **Gauge pressure** — Pressure relative to local atmospheric pressure.
- **Absolute pressure** — Pressure measured relative to a perfect vacuum.
- **Differential pressure** — Difference between two pressures.
- **Atmospheric pressure** — Pressure exerted by the surrounding atmosphere.
- **Temperature scale** — Framework for expressing temperature, such as Celsius, Fahrenheit, or Kelvin.
- **Tolerance** — Permitted variation from a specified value.
- **Metrology** — Science of measurement.
- **Measurement uncertainty** — Quantified range or parameter describing doubt in a measurement result.

## 4\. Engineering materials and material science

- **Material science** — Study of the structure, properties, processing, and performance of materials.
- **Ferrous metal** — Metal or alloy primarily based on iron.
- **Nonferrous metal** — Metal or alloy not primarily based on iron.
- **Alloy** — Material containing multiple elements, at least one of which is metallic.
- **Microstructure** — Arrangement of grains, phases, and defects within a material.
- **Crystal structure** — Ordered atomic arrangement in a crystalline solid.
- **Grain** — Region of a crystalline material with a particular crystal orientation.
- **Grain boundary** — Interface between adjacent grains.
- **Phase** — Region of material with a distinct structure and composition.
- **Phase diagram** — Diagram showing stable phases under specified conditions.
- **Iron-carbon diagram** — Phase diagram used to understand steels and cast irons.
- **Ferrite** — Relatively soft, low-carbon iron-based phase in steel.
- **Austenite** — Face-centered cubic phase of iron important in steel processing.
- **Cementite** — Iron carbide, Fe₃C, found in many steels and cast irons.
- **Pearlite** — Microstructure consisting of alternating ferrite and cementite.
- **Martensite** — Hard phase formed by rapid cooling of suitable steels.
- **Bainite** — Steel microstructure formed through specific transformation conditions.
- **Ductility** — Ability to undergo plastic deformation before fracture.
- **Malleability** — Ability to deform plastically under compressive loading.
- **Brittleness** — Tendency to fracture with limited plastic deformation.
- **Elasticity** — Ability to recover deformation after unloading.
- **Plasticity** — Ability to retain permanent deformation.
- **Strength** — Ability to withstand a specified type of loading without failure.
- **Stiffness** — Resistance to elastic deformation.
- **Hardness** — Resistance to localized indentation, scratching, or penetration.
- **Toughness** — Ability to absorb energy before fracture.
- **Resilience** — Energy absorbed elastically before yielding.
- **Young's modulus** — Ratio of normal stress to normal strain in the linear elastic range.
- **Shear modulus** — Ratio of shear stress to shear strain in the linear elastic range.
- **Bulk modulus** — Measure of resistance to uniform compression.
- **Poisson's ratio** — Ratio of lateral strain to axial strain magnitude under uniaxial loading.
- **Yield strength** — Stress at which specified plastic deformation begins.
- **Ultimate tensile strength (UTS)** — Maximum engineering tensile stress reached in a tensile test.
- **Fracture strength** — Stress associated with fracture under specified test conditions.
- **Compressive strength** — Resistance to compressive loading.
- **Shear strength** — Resistance to shear failure.
- **Fatigue strength** — Stress level associated with a specified fatigue life and test condition.
- **Creep** — Time-dependent deformation under sustained loading.
- **Stress relaxation** — Reduction in stress over time under constrained deformation.
- **Strain hardening** — Increase in strength caused by plastic deformation.
- **Work hardening** — Another term for strain hardening.
- **Annealing** — Heat treatment used to soften a material or modify its structure and residual stresses.
- **Normalizing** — Heat treatment commonly used to refine the microstructure of steel.
- **Quenching** — Rapid cooling after heating.
- **Tempering** — Heat treatment after hardening to adjust properties and reduce brittleness.
- **Case hardening** — Producing a hard surface with a comparatively softer interior.
- **Carburizing** — Increasing carbon content near a steel surface.
- **Nitriding** — Introducing nitrogen into a steel surface to improve surface properties.
- **Induction hardening** — Surface hardening using electromagnetic induction heating followed by quenching.
- **Weldability** — Suitability of a material for producing an acceptable weld.
- **Machinability** — Ease of machining a material to the required specification.
- **Corrosion** — Deterioration caused by chemical or electrochemical reactions.
- **Oxidation** — Reaction involving electron loss or, commonly in engineering, reaction with oxygen.
- **Galvanic corrosion** — Accelerated corrosion that can occur when dissimilar conductive materials are electrically connected in an electrolyte.
- **Passivation** — Formation or maintenance of a protective surface film.
- **Thermal expansion** — Change in dimensions due to temperature change.
- **Thermal conductivity** — Ability to conduct heat.
- **Electrical conductivity** — Ability to conduct electric current.
- **Specific heat capacity** — Energy required per unit mass to raise temperature by one degree.
- **Density** — Mass per unit volume.
- **Porosity** — Fraction or degree of void space within a material.
- **Composite material** — Material made from distinct constituents combined for desired properties.
- **Fiber-reinforced polymer (FRP)** — Polymer reinforced with fibers such as glass or carbon.
- **Ceramic** — Inorganic, nonmetallic material generally processed at elevated temperatures.
- **Polymer** — Material made of long molecular chains or networks.
- **Thermoplastic** — Polymer that can generally be softened by heating and reshaped.
- **Thermoset** — Polymer that forms a cross-linked network and cannot be remelted like a typical thermoplastic.
- **Elastomer** — Polymer capable of substantial elastic deformation.
- **Shape memory alloy** — Alloy capable of recovering a trained shape under suitable conditions.
- **Smart material** — Material that responds usefully to environmental or applied stimuli.
- **Fracture toughness** — Resistance to crack propagation under specified conditions.
- **Residual stress** — Stress remaining in a material without external loading.
- **Delamination** — Separation between layers in a layered material or composite.
- **Heat-affected zone (HAZ)** — Material near a weld that experiences thermal changes without melting.
- **Embrittlement** — Loss of ductility or toughness.
- **Hydrogen embrittlement** — Loss of ductility or cracking associated with hydrogen in susceptible materials.
- **Stress corrosion cracking** — Cracking caused by the combined influence of tensile stress and a corrosive environment.

## 5\. Strength of materials and solid mechanics

- **Normal stress** — Force per unit area acting perpendicular to a cross-section.
- **Shear stress** — Force per unit area acting tangentially to a surface.
- **Engineering stress** — Load divided by original cross-sectional area.
- **True stress** — Stress calculated using the instantaneous cross-sectional area.
- **Engineering strain** — Change in length divided by original length.
- **True strain** — Strain measure based on incremental deformation relative to current length.
- **Axial loading** — Loading along a member's longitudinal axis.
- **Tensile loading** — Loading that pulls a member apart.
- **Compressive loading** — Loading that pushes a member together.
- **Torsion** — Twisting of a member under torque.
- **Bending** — Deformation caused by bending moments.
- **Transverse shear** — Shear associated with forces acting transverse to a member.
- **Shear flow** — Shear force transmitted per unit length, commonly used for thin-walled structures.
- **Hooke's law** — Linear relationship between stress and strain within the elastic range.
- **Stress-strain curve** — Graph describing a material's response to loading.
- **Proportional limit** — Limit below which stress and strain remain proportional.
- **Elastic limit** — Approximate limit of recoverable deformation.
- **Yield point** — Stress level at which yielding becomes evident in certain materials.
- **Offset yield strength** — Yield strength determined using a specified offset strain, often 0.2%.
- **Modulus of elasticity** — Another name for Young's modulus in uniaxial loading.
- **Elastic deformation** — Recoverable deformation.
- **Plastic deformation** — Permanent deformation.
- **Axial deformation** — Change in length due to axial loading.
- **Beam** — Structural member primarily designed to resist transverse loads and bending.
- **Cantilever beam** — Beam fixed at one end and free at the other.
- **Simply supported beam** — Beam supported at two ends with idealized support conditions.
- **Fixed support** — Support that prevents specified translation and rotation.
- **Pin support** — Idealized support that prevents translation while permitting rotation.
- **Roller support** — Idealized support that allows motion in one direction while constraining another.
- **Shear-force diagram (SFD)** — Plot of internal shear force along a beam.
- **Bending-moment diagram (BMD)** — Plot of internal bending moment along a beam.
- **Deflection** — Displacement of a structural member from its original position.
- **Slope of a beam** — Angular rotation of the beam's deflected centerline.
- **Flexural rigidity** — Product of Young's modulus and second moment of area, $EI$.
- **Neutral axis** — Line or surface in a bent member where longitudinal bending strain is zero.
- **Section modulus** — Geometric property relating bending moment to maximum bending stress.
- **Polar moment of inertia** — Geometric property describing a cross-section's distribution of area about an axis, commonly used for circular-shaft torsion.
- **Thin-walled pressure vessel** — Vessel whose wall thickness is small relative to its radius.
- **Hoop stress** — Circumferential stress in a pressure vessel.
- **Longitudinal stress** — Axial stress in a pressure vessel.
- **Buckling** — Instability involving sudden lateral deformation under compressive loading.
- **Euler buckling** — Ideal elastic buckling model for slender columns.
- **Slenderness ratio** — Measure comparing a column's effective length with its radius of gyration.
- **Effective length** — Equivalent buckling length based on end restraints.
- **Column** — Member primarily subjected to compression.
- **Strut** — Compression-carrying member.
- **Stress concentration** — Local increase in stress caused by geometric or material discontinuities.
- **Stress concentration factor** — Ratio of peak local stress to a nominal stress.
- **Notch sensitivity** — Degree to which a material's fatigue performance is affected by notches.
- **Principal stress** — Normal stress acting on a plane with zero shear stress.
- **Principal plane** — Plane on which shear stress is zero.
- **Mohr's circle** — Graphical method for transforming stress or strain components.
- **Von Mises stress** — Equivalent stress used to assess yielding in many ductile materials.
- **Tresca criterion** — Yield criterion based on maximum shear stress.
- **Combined loading** — Simultaneous action of multiple load types.
- **Superposition** — Combining responses to individual loads when the system is linear.
- **Strain energy** — Energy stored by deformation.
- **Castigliano's theorem** — Method for finding displacements from derivatives of strain energy.
- **Statically indeterminate structure** — Structure requiring compatibility and deformation relationships in addition to equilibrium.
- **Compatibility condition** — Requirement that connected parts deform consistently.
- **Fatigue** — Progressive damage under repeated or fluctuating loading.
- **S-N curve** — Plot of stress amplitude against cycles to failure.
- **Endurance limit** — Stress amplitude below which certain materials exhibit a fatigue limit under specified conditions.
- **Crack propagation** — Growth of an existing crack.
- **Paris law** — Empirical relationship between fatigue crack-growth rate and stress-intensity-factor range.
- **Stress intensity factor** — Parameter describing the severity of a crack-tip stress field.
- **Fracture mechanics** — Study of cracks, crack growth, and fracture.
- **Low-cycle fatigue** — Fatigue involving relatively large cyclic strains and generally fewer cycles.
- **High-cycle fatigue** — Fatigue involving many cycles, often with predominantly elastic strain.
- **Creep rupture** — Failure after time-dependent deformation under sustained loading.
- **Contact stress** — Stress arising from forces between contacting surfaces.
- **Hertzian contact** — Elastic contact analysis for curved surfaces.
- **Wear** — Material loss or surface damage from contact and relative motion.
- **Tribology** — Study of friction, lubrication, and wear.

## 6\. Thermodynamics

- **Thermodynamics** — Study of heat, work, energy, and their transformations.
- **System** — The portion of the universe selected for analysis.
- **Surroundings** — Everything outside the chosen system.
- **Boundary** — Real or imaginary surface separating a system from its surroundings.
- **Closed system** — System that exchanges energy but not mass with its surroundings.
- **Open system** — System that can exchange mass and energy with its surroundings.
- **Isolated system** — Ideal system that exchanges neither mass nor energy.
- **Control volume** — Defined region through which mass and energy may flow.
- **State** — Condition of a system described by its properties.
- **Property** — Measurable or calculable characteristic of a system.
- **State variable** — Variable describing the state of a system.
- **State function** — Property depending on state rather than the path taken.
- **Process** — Change from one thermodynamic state to another.
- **Cycle** — Sequence of processes returning a system to its initial state.
- **Equation of state** — Relationship between thermodynamic properties.
- **Ideal gas** — Model gas obeying $PV=mRT$.
- **Real gas** — Gas whose behavior can deviate from the ideal-gas model.
- **Pressure** — Normal force per unit area.
- **Temperature** — Property related to thermal equilibrium and the direction of heat transfer.
- **Internal energy** — Microscopic energy stored within a substance.
- **Enthalpy** — Property defined as $H=U+PV$.
- **Entropy** — Thermodynamic property used in energy-quality analysis and the second law.
- **Specific volume** — Volume per unit mass.
- **Specific heat** — Heat capacity expressed per unit mass, often specified at constant pressure or volume.
- **Heat** — Energy transferred because of a temperature difference.
- **Work transfer** — Energy transferred through a work interaction.
- **Sensible heat** — Energy transfer associated with temperature change without a phase change in the idealized process.
- **Latent heat** — Energy associated with a phase change at specified conditions.
- **First law of thermodynamics** — Conservation of energy.
- **Second law of thermodynamics** — Governs entropy generation and the direction of natural processes.
- **Zeroth law of thermodynamics** — Establishes the transitive nature of thermal equilibrium.
- **Third law of thermodynamics** — Describes the limiting behavior of entropy as temperature approaches absolute zero for an ideal perfect crystal.
- **Isothermal process** — Process at constant temperature.
- **Isobaric process** — Process at constant pressure.
- **Isochoric process** — Process at constant volume.
- **Isometric process** — Another term sometimes used for constant-volume processes.
- **Adiabatic process** — Process with no heat transfer across the system boundary.
- **Isentropic process** — Process at constant entropy.
- **Polytropic process** — Process modeled by a relationship such as $PV^n=C$.
- **Quasistatic process** — Idealized process proceeding through near-equilibrium states.
- **Reversible process** — Ideal process that can be reversed without leaving net changes in the system and surroundings.
- **Irreversibility** — Departure from reversible behavior, commonly associated with entropy generation.
- **Thermal equilibrium** — State in which no net heat transfer occurs due to temperature differences.
- **Thermal efficiency** — Useful work output divided by heat input for an applicable heat-engine cycle.
- **Coefficient of performance (COP)** — Heating or cooling delivered divided by work input.
- **Heat engine** — Device that converts heat into work while operating in a cycle.
- **Refrigerator** — Device that removes heat from a low-temperature region using work input.
- **Heat pump** — Device that supplies heat to a region using work input.
- **Carnot cycle** — Ideal reversible cycle establishing limits on thermal efficiency or COP.
- **Otto cycle** — Idealized cycle used to model spark-ignition engines.
- **Diesel cycle** — Idealized cycle used to model compression-ignition engines.
- **Dual cycle** — Idealized cycle combining constant-volume and constant-pressure heat addition.
- **Brayton cycle** — Idealized gas-turbine cycle.
- **Rankine cycle** — Idealized vapor power cycle used in steam power plants.
- **Vapor-compression cycle** — Refrigeration cycle using compression, condensation, expansion, and evaporation.
- **Psychrometrics** — Study of moist-air properties.
- **Relative humidity** — Ratio of actual water-vapor pressure to saturation vapor pressure at the same temperature.
- **Dew point** — Temperature at which air becomes saturated when cooled at the specified pressure and moisture content.
- **Wet-bulb temperature** — Temperature measured or defined through evaporative cooling under specified conditions.
- **Dry-bulb temperature** — Ordinary air temperature measured by a suitably exposed thermometer.
- **Superheated vapor** — Vapor above its saturation temperature at a given pressure.
- **Subcooled liquid** — Liquid below its saturation temperature at a given pressure.
- **Saturated liquid** — Liquid at the state where vaporization is about to begin.
- **Saturated vapor** — Vapor at the state where condensation is about to begin.
- **Quality (dryness fraction)** — Mass fraction of vapor in a saturated liquid-vapor mixture.
- **Critical point** — State where the liquid-vapor distinction disappears.
- **Triple point** — Conditions at which three phases coexist in equilibrium.
- **Throttling process** — Flow process modeled with approximately constant enthalpy.
- **Joule-Thomson effect** — Temperature change associated with throttling a real gas.
- **Combustion** — Chemical reaction releasing energy, commonly through oxidation.
- **Stoichiometric ratio** — Reactant ratio corresponding to complete reaction under the specified chemical model.
- **Air-fuel ratio (AFR)** — Mass of air divided by mass of fuel.
- **Excess air** — Air supplied beyond the stoichiometric requirement.
- **Calorific value** — Energy released per unit quantity of fuel during complete combustion under specified conditions.
- **Higher heating value (HHV)** — Fuel heating value accounting for recovery of water-vapor condensation energy.
- **Lower heating value (LHV)** — Fuel heating value excluding recovery of that condensation energy.
- **Exergy** — Maximum useful work obtainable as a system comes into equilibrium with a defined environment.
- **Exergy destruction** — Loss of work potential caused by irreversibility.

## 7\. Heat transfer

- **Heat transfer** — Movement of thermal energy due to a temperature difference.
- **Conduction** — Heat transfer through matter via microscopic interactions.
- **Convection** — Heat transfer between a surface and a moving fluid.
- **Natural convection** — Fluid motion driven primarily by buoyancy effects.
- **Forced convection** — Fluid motion driven by a fan, pump, or other external mechanism.
- **Radiation** — Energy transfer through electromagnetic waves.
- **Thermal resistance** — Opposition to heat transfer.
- **Thermal circuit** — Equivalent resistance-network representation of heat transfer.
- **Thermal conductivity,****$k$** — Material property describing conductive heat transfer.
- **Heat flux** — Heat-transfer rate per unit area.
- **Temperature gradient** — Spatial rate of temperature change.
- **Fourier's law** — Relationship between conductive heat flux and temperature gradient.
- **Newton's law of cooling** — Convective heat-transfer model using a heat-transfer coefficient.
- **Stefan-Boltzmann law** — Relationship between thermal radiation and absolute temperature to the fourth power for an ideal blackbody.
- **Emissivity** — Measure of a surface's emission relative to a blackbody.
- **Absorptivity** — Fraction of incident radiation absorbed.
- **Reflectivity** — Fraction of incident radiation reflected.
- **Transmissivity** — Fraction of incident radiation transmitted.
- **Blackbody** — Ideal surface that absorbs all incident radiation and emits the maximum possible thermal radiation at a given temperature.
- **View factor** — Geometric factor describing radiation exchange between surfaces.
- **Overall heat-transfer coefficient,****$U$** — Combined coefficient representing multiple heat-transfer resistances.
- **Log mean temperature difference (LMTD)** — Effective average temperature difference used in heat-exchanger analysis.
- **Heat exchanger** — Device that transfers heat between fluids.
- **Parallel-flow heat exchanger** — Exchanger in which fluids move in the same general direction.
- **Counterflow heat exchanger** — Exchanger in which fluids move in opposite directions.
- **Crossflow heat exchanger** — Exchanger in which fluid streams cross at an angle, typically near 90 degrees.
- **Effectiveness** — Actual heat transfer divided by the maximum possible heat transfer for a specified inlet state.
- **NTU method** — Heat-exchanger analysis method based on the number of transfer units and capacity-rate ratio.
- **Thermal diffusivity** — Ratio of thermal conductivity to volumetric heat capacity.
- **Biot number** — Ratio of internal conduction resistance to external convection resistance in a characteristic model.
- **Fourier number** — Dimensionless time used in transient heat conduction.
- **Nusselt number** — Dimensionless measure of convective relative to conductive heat transfer.
- **Prandtl number** — Ratio of momentum diffusivity to thermal diffusivity.
- **Rayleigh number** — Dimensionless parameter characterizing buoyancy-driven convection.
- **Grashof number** — Ratio comparing buoyancy and viscous effects in natural convection.
- **Transient heat transfer** — Heat transfer with temperature changing over time.
- **Steady-state heat transfer** — Heat transfer under conditions where temperatures at fixed locations do not change with time.
- **Thermal contact resistance** — Resistance to heat flow between contacting surfaces.
- **Fin** — Extended surface used to increase heat-transfer area.
- **Fin efficiency** — Actual fin heat transfer divided by the ideal maximum at the base temperature.
- **Heat sink** — Component designed to dissipate heat to its surroundings.
- **Thermal runaway** — Self-reinforcing temperature rise when heat generation increases faster than heat removal.
- **Phase-change material (PCM)** — Material used to store or release thermal energy through a phase change.
- **Thermal management** — Controlling heat generation, transfer, and dissipation to maintain acceptable temperatures.

## 8\. Fluid mechanics

- **Fluid** — Substance that continuously deforms under applied shear stress.
- **Fluid statics** — Study of fluids at rest.
- **Fluid dynamics** — Study of moving fluids.
- **Continuum assumption** — Model treating matter as continuously distributed rather than tracking individual molecules.
- **Density** — Mass per unit volume.
- **Specific weight** — Weight per unit volume.
- **Specific gravity** — Ratio of a substance's density to a reference density.
- **Viscosity** — Measure of a fluid's resistance to shearing flow.
- **Dynamic viscosity** — Proportionality between shear stress and velocity gradient for a Newtonian fluid.
- **Kinematic viscosity** — Dynamic viscosity divided by density.
- **Newtonian fluid** — Fluid whose shear stress is proportional to shear rate under the standard model.
- **Non-Newtonian fluid** — Fluid whose shear stress does not follow the simple Newtonian relationship.
- **Compressible flow** — Flow in which density changes are significant.
- **Incompressible flow** — Flow modeled with negligible density changes.
- **Steady flow** — Flow properties at a fixed location do not vary with time.
- **Unsteady flow** — Flow properties vary with time.
- **Laminar flow** — Flow dominated by orderly layers with limited mixing across them.
- **Turbulent flow** — Flow featuring fluctuating motion and enhanced mixing.
- **Reynolds number** — Dimensionless ratio of inertial to viscous effects.
- **Mach number** — Ratio of flow speed to local speed of sound.
- **Froude number** — Dimensionless ratio relating inertia to gravity effects.
- **Strouhal number** — Dimensionless measure relating oscillation frequency to flow speed and characteristic length.
- **Volumetric flow rate** — Volume of fluid passing a section per unit time.
- **Mass flow rate** — Mass of fluid passing a section per unit time.
- **Velocity profile** — Distribution of flow velocity across a section.
- **Boundary layer** — Region near a surface where viscous effects are important.
- **Boundary-layer separation** — Detachment of a boundary layer from a surface, often due to an adverse pressure gradient.
- **No-slip condition** — Idealized condition in which fluid velocity at a stationary solid surface equals the surface velocity.
- **Pressure head** — Pressure expressed as an equivalent height of fluid.
- **Velocity head** — Kinetic energy per unit weight expressed as a height.
- **Elevation head** — Gravitational potential energy per unit weight expressed as a height.
- **Hydrostatic pressure** — Pressure variation in a fluid at rest due to gravity.
- **Pascal's law** — Pressure changes applied to a confined fluid are transmitted throughout it in the idealized static model.
- **Archimedes' principle** — Buoyant force equals the weight of displaced fluid.
- **Buoyancy** — Upward or otherwise opposing gravitational force exerted by a surrounding fluid.
- **Bernoulli's equation** — Energy relationship along a streamline under specified assumptions.
- **Continuity equation** — Mass conservation relationship for fluid flow.
- **Momentum equation** — Relationship describing the rate of momentum change and applied forces.
- **Navier-Stokes equations** — Governing equations for fluid motion incorporating momentum conservation and viscous stresses.
- **Euler equation** — Idealized fluid momentum equation neglecting viscosity.
- **Pitot tube** — Instrument used to infer flow velocity from pressure measurements.
- **Venturi meter** — Flow-measurement device using pressure differences across a constriction.
- **Orifice plate** — Restriction used to estimate flow from a pressure difference.
- **Rotameter** — Variable-area flow meter using a float.
- **Manometer** — Instrument that measures pressure using a liquid column.
- **Differential pressure sensor** — Instrument measuring the pressure difference between two points.
- **Drag** — Fluid force opposing relative motion in the direction of flow.
- **Lift** — Fluid force perpendicular to the relative flow direction.
- **Drag coefficient** — Dimensionless coefficient characterizing drag under specified conditions.
- **Lift coefficient** — Dimensionless coefficient characterizing lift under specified conditions.
- **Pressure coefficient** — Dimensionless representation of local pressure relative to a reference flow condition.
- **Cavitation** — Formation and collapse of vapor cavities when local pressure falls sufficiently low.
- **Water hammer** — Pressure surge caused by rapid changes in liquid flow velocity.
- **Head loss** — Reduction in mechanical energy per unit weight caused by flow resistance.
- **Major loss** — Flow energy loss along straight pipe due to friction.
- **Minor loss** — Additional loss caused by fittings, valves, entrances, exits, and geometry changes.
- **Darcy-Weisbach equation** — Equation for pressure or head loss due to pipe friction.
- **Moody chart** — Chart used to estimate the Darcy friction factor.
- **Darcy friction factor** — Dimensionless coefficient used in Darcy-Weisbach calculations.
- **Hydraulic diameter** — Equivalent diameter used for flow through noncircular ducts.
- **Relative roughness** — Pipe roughness height divided by internal diameter.
- **Pump** — Machine that adds mechanical energy to a fluid.
- **Turbine** — Machine that extracts energy from a fluid.
- **Pump head** — Energy added by a pump per unit weight of fluid.
- **System curve** — Relationship between required pumping head and flow rate.
- **Pump curve** — Relationship between pump performance variables such as head, flow, and efficiency.
- **Operating point** — Intersection of the pump curve and system curve.
- **Net positive suction head (NPSH)** — Measure used to evaluate suction-side pressure conditions relative to vapor pressure.
- **NPSH available (NPSHa)** — Suction pressure margin calculated for the installed system.
- **NPSH required (NPSHr)** — Manufacturer-specified suction condition associated with a stated pump-performance criterion.
- **Priming** — Filling a pump or suction line with liquid so pumping can begin as intended.
- **Positive-displacement pump** — Pump that moves fluid by repeatedly trapping and displacing a volume.
- **Centrifugal pump** — Rotodynamic pump that transfers energy using a rotating impeller.
- **Impeller** — Rotating component that transfers energy to fluid in many pumps and turbines.
- **Volute** — Spiral-shaped casing used to collect flow from an impeller.
- **Diffuser** — Passage that decelerates flow and converts kinetic energy into pressure.
- **Hydraulic efficiency** — Measure of useful hydraulic output relative to input under a specified definition.
- **Pump affinity laws** — Approximate scaling relationships between speed, flow, head, and power for geometrically similar operation.
- **Hydraulic circuit** — Arrangement of components controlling liquid power transmission.
- **Hydraulic actuator** — Device that converts hydraulic energy into mechanical motion.
- **Hydraulic accumulator** — Device storing pressurized hydraulic energy.
- **Pneumatics** — Technology using compressed gas to transmit power or control motion.

## 9\. Machine design and mechanical components

- **Machine element** — Individual component serving a mechanical function.
- **Mechanism** — Arrangement of components that produces or transmits motion.
- **Kinematic pair** — Connection between bodies that permits a defined relative motion.
- **Degree of freedom (DOF)** — Number of independent coordinates needed to describe a system's configuration.
- **Linkage** — Connected rigid or flexible members used to transmit or transform motion.
- **Four-bar linkage** — Mechanism consisting of four links connected by four joints.
- **Slider-crank mechanism** — Mechanism converting rotary motion to reciprocating motion or vice versa.
- **Cam** — Shaped component that controls follower motion.
- **Follower** — Component that tracks a cam profile.
- **Gear** — Toothed element transmitting rotational motion and torque.
- **Gear ratio** — Ratio relating rotational speeds or tooth counts for a defined gear arrangement.
- **Gear train** — Multiple interconnected gears.
- **Spur gear** — Gear with straight teeth parallel to its axis.
- **Helical gear** — Gear with angled teeth.
- **Bevel gear** — Gear designed to transmit motion between intersecting shafts.
- **Worm gear** — Gear arrangement commonly used for large speed reductions between nonparallel, nonintersecting shafts.
- **Rack and pinion** — Gear pair converting rotary motion to linear motion or vice versa.
- **Planetary gearset** — Gear arrangement with planet gears revolving around a central sun gear.
- **Sun gear** — Central gear in a planetary gearset.
- **Ring gear** — Outer gear in many planetary gearsets.
- **Planet carrier** — Component supporting the planet gear shafts.
- **Backlash** — Clearance between mating gear teeth.
- **Gear module** — Metric measure of gear tooth size.
- **Diametral pitch** — Number of gear teeth per unit pitch diameter in inch-based gear systems.
- **Pitch circle** — Reference circle used to define gear geometry and motion.
- **Pressure angle** — Angle defining the direction of tooth contact force relative to the tangent at the pitch point.
- **Involute profile** — Common gear tooth profile generated from an unwinding taut string.
- **Helix angle** — Angle between a helical tooth and the gear axis.
- **Contact ratio** — Average number of tooth pairs in contact during gear engagement.
- **Bearing** — Component that supports and guides relative motion.
- **Rolling-element bearing** — Bearing using balls or rollers between raceways.
- **Ball bearing** — Bearing using spherical rolling elements.
- **Roller bearing** — Bearing using cylindrical, tapered, spherical, or other roller elements.
- **Thrust bearing** — Bearing designed primarily for axial loads.
- **Journal bearing** — Sliding bearing supporting a shaft on a journal surface.
- **Plain bearing** — Bearing with sliding rather than rolling contact.
- **Bearing clearance** — Internal space or play between bearing components.
- **Bearing preload** — Intentional internal loading to reduce play or increase stiffness.
- **Bearing life** — Expected service duration under specified operating conditions and statistical assumptions.
- **L10 life** — Bearing life that 90% of a sufficiently large group of identical bearings are expected to reach or exceed under specified conditions.
- **Lubrication** — Introducing a substance to reduce friction, wear, or heat.
- **Lubricant viscosity** — Resistance of a lubricant to flow and shear.
- **Viscosity index** — Measure of how strongly a lubricant's viscosity changes with temperature.
- **Grease lubrication** — Lubrication using a semisolid mixture of base oil, thickener, and additives.
- **Hydrodynamic lubrication** — Lubrication where relative motion creates a load-supporting fluid film.
- **Hydrostatic lubrication** — Lubrication where externally pressurized fluid supports the load.
- **Elastohydrodynamic lubrication (EHL)** — Lubrication regime involving elastic deformation and pressure-dependent lubricant properties.
- **Boundary lubrication** — Regime in which surface interactions and thin films carry much of the load.
- **Shaft** — Rotating or stationary member supporting components and transmitting torque.
- **Axle** — Member supporting rotating components, not necessarily transmitting torque.
- **Key** — Element that prevents relative rotation between a shaft and a mounted component.
- **Keyway** — Slot receiving a key.
- **Spline** — Multiple ridges or teeth transmitting torque between mating components.
- **Coupling** — Device connecting two shafts.
- **Rigid coupling** — Coupling designed to connect aligned shafts with minimal flexibility.
- **Flexible coupling** — Coupling that accommodates specified misalignment or movement.
- **Universal joint** — Joint transmitting rotation between shafts at an angle.
- **Clutch** — Device that engages or disengages power transmission.
- **Brake** — Device that slows or stops motion by dissipating or otherwise managing energy.
- **Flywheel** — Rotating mass that stores kinetic energy and smooths speed fluctuations.
- **Governor** — Device that regulates speed or another operating variable.
- **Spring** — Element that stores and releases elastic energy.
- **Compression spring** — Spring designed to resist compressive displacement.
- **Extension spring** — Spring designed to resist extension.
- **Torsion spring** — Spring that produces a torque in response to angular displacement.
- **Spring rate** — Force per unit displacement or torque per unit angular displacement, depending on the spring.
- **Damping** — Dissipation of mechanical energy.
- **Damper** — Component that resists motion and dissipates energy.
- **Shock absorber** — Device designed to control transient motion and dissipate energy.
- **Fastener** — Hardware used to join components.
- **Bolt** — Externally threaded fastener commonly used with a nut or threaded hole.
- **Screw** — Threaded fastener that engages a mating thread or forms its own thread in certain applications.
- **Stud** — Rod threaded at one or both ends, used for fastening.
- **Nut** — Internally threaded fastener.
- **Washer** — Component used under a fastener to distribute load, protect surfaces, or serve another specified function.
- **Thread pitch** — Distance between corresponding points on adjacent threads.
- **Lead** — Axial distance advanced in one revolution of a screw.
- **Thread engagement** — Length or extent over which mating threads interact.
- **Preload** — Initial force applied to a joint or component before external service loading.
- **Bolt proof load** — Specified load associated with a fastener's proof strength.
- **Joint separation** — Loss of contact between clamped members under loading.
- **Fatigue failure of fasteners** — Failure from cyclic loading, often influenced by stress concentration and preload.
- **Interference fit** — Fit in which the mating parts have overlapping dimensional ranges that require force or thermal assistance for assembly.
- **Clearance fit** — Fit designed to provide space between mating parts.
- **Transition fit** — Fit whose tolerances can produce either slight clearance or slight interference.
- **Press fit** — Interference fit assembled by applying force.
- **Shrink fit** — Interference fit assembled using thermal expansion or contraction.
- **Tolerance stack-up** — Combined effect of dimensional variations across an assembly.
- **O-ring** — Elastomeric ring used to seal a joint.
- **Gasket** — Sealing component placed between mating surfaces.
- **Mechanical seal** — Device sealing around a rotating shaft using contacting or closely controlled faces.
- **Lip seal** — Seal using a flexible lip against a shaft or other surface.
- **Bellows** — Flexible component that accommodates movement while containing or isolating a medium.
- **Diaphragm** — Flexible membrane used to separate regions or transmit pressure and force.
- **Valve** — Device that controls fluid flow.
- **Check valve** — Valve intended to permit flow in one direction and restrict reverse flow.
- **Relief valve** — Valve that limits pressure by opening under specified conditions.
- **Pressure regulator** — Device that controls downstream pressure.
- **Solenoid valve** — Electrically actuated valve using a solenoid.
- **Ball valve** — Valve that controls flow with a rotating ball.
- **Gate valve** — Valve using a sliding gate-like element.
- **Globe valve** — Valve with a movable plug-like element used to regulate or stop flow.
- **Butterfly valve** — Valve using a rotating disk.
- **Actuator** — Device that produces mechanical movement from an energy source.
- **Linear actuator** — Actuator that produces linear motion.
- **Rotary actuator** — Actuator that produces rotational motion.
- **Mechanical advantage** — Ratio of output force to input force.
- **Transmission ratio** — Relationship between input and output motion or speed.
- **Torque transmission** — Transfer of twisting moment through a mechanical system.
- **Power transmission** — Transfer of mechanical power between components.
- **Service factor** — Adjustment factor accounting for specified operating conditions or duty.
- **Duty cycle** — Pattern of operating and resting periods over time.
- **Design life** — Intended service duration or number of cycles under specified conditions.
- **Maintainability** — Ease with which a system can be inspected, repaired, or restored.
- **Reliability** — Probability that a system performs its required function for a specified time under stated conditions.

## 10\. Manufacturing and machining

- **Manufacturing process** — Method used to produce a component or product.
- **Casting** — Producing a part by pouring or injecting material into a mold.
- **Sand casting** — Casting using a sand-based mold.
- **Die casting** — Forcing molten metal into a reusable die under pressure.
- **Investment casting** — Casting process using a sacrificial pattern to create a detailed mold.
- **Centrifugal casting** — Casting in which centrifugal force distributes molten material.
- **Molding** — Forming material within a mold cavity.
- **Forging** — Shaping material using compressive forces.
- **Open-die forging** — Forging between dies that do not fully enclose the workpiece.
- **Closed-die forging** — Forging within shaped die cavities.
- **Rolling** — Reducing or shaping material by passing it between rolls.
- **Extrusion** — Forcing material through a die to create a continuous cross-section.
- **Drawing** — Pulling material through a die to reduce its cross-section or shape it.
- **Sheet-metal forming** — Shaping sheet through bending, stretching, drawing, or related processes.
- **Deep drawing** — Forming sheet metal into a cup-like or hollow shape using a punch and die.
- **Stamping** — Forming or cutting sheet material using presses and tooling.
- **Blanking** — Cutting a desired flat shape from sheet stock.
- **Punching** — Creating a hole by shearing material.
- **Shearing** — Cutting material through opposing forces.
- **Bending allowance** — Developed length allocated for the curved portion of a bend.
- **Springback** — Elastic recovery after forming, which changes the final geometry.
- **Machining** — Removing material to produce a specified shape.
- **Subtractive manufacturing** — Manufacturing by removing material from stock.
- **Additive manufacturing** — Building parts by adding material, commonly layer by layer.
- **CNC machining** — Computer numerical control of machine tools.
- **CNC milling** — Milling controlled by numerical instructions.
- **CNC turning** — Turning controlled by numerical instructions.
- **Milling** — Machining with a rotating cutting tool.
- **Turning** — Machining in which the workpiece typically rotates relative to a cutting tool.
- **Drilling** — Producing a round hole with a drill.
- **Boring** — Enlarging or correcting an existing hole with a cutting tool.
- **Reaming** — Finishing a previously formed hole to improve size and surface quality.
- **Tapping** — Producing internal threads with a tap.
- **Thread milling** — Producing threads with a milling tool.
- **Grinding** — Abrasive machining for material removal or surface finishing.
- **Surface grinding** — Grinding a surface to achieve specified geometry and finish.
- **Cylindrical grinding** — Grinding external or internal cylindrical surfaces.
- **Honing** — Abrasive finishing process often used to improve bore geometry and surface texture.
- **Lapping** — Fine finishing using loose or bonded abrasive action.
- **Broaching** — Machining using a tool with progressively changing cutting edges.
- **Sawing** — Cutting material with a toothed blade.
- **EDM (electrical discharge machining)** — Material removal using controlled electrical discharges.
- **Wire EDM** — EDM using a moving wire electrode.
- **Laser cutting** — Cutting with a focused laser beam.
- **Waterjet cutting** — Cutting using a high-pressure fluid jet, sometimes with abrasive particles.
- **Plasma cutting** — Cutting electrically conductive materials using a plasma jet.
- **Ultrasonic machining** — Material removal using ultrasonic vibration and abrasive action.
- **Tool geometry** — Shape and orientation of cutting-tool features.
- **Rake angle** — Angle describing the orientation of a cutting tool's rake face.
- **Relief angle** — Angle providing clearance between the tool flank and the machined surface.
- **Cutting speed** — Relative surface speed between the cutting edge and workpiece.
- **Feed rate** — Rate at which the tool advances relative to the workpiece.
- **Feed per tooth** — Distance advanced per cutting tooth engagement in milling.
- **Depth of cut** — Thickness or depth of material removed in a pass.
- **Material removal rate (MRR)** — Volume of material removed per unit time.
- **Chip formation** — Process by which material separates during cutting.
- **Continuous chip** — Unbroken or nearly continuous chip formed in cutting.
- **Discontinuous chip** — Chip separated into segments.
- **Built-up edge (BUE)** — Workpiece material adhering to a cutting edge during machining.
- **Tool wear** — Degradation of cutting-tool geometry or surface.
- **Flank wear** — Wear on the tool flank contacting the machined surface.
- **Crater wear** — Wear depression on the tool's rake face.
- **Tool life** — Operating time or cutting distance before a defined tool-wear or failure criterion is reached.
- **Coolant** — Fluid used to remove heat, lubricate, flush chips, or perform other machining functions.
- **Cutting fluid** — Fluid applied during machining for cooling and/or lubrication.
- **Workholding** — Methods used to locate and secure a workpiece.
- **Fixture** — Device that locates and holds a workpiece.
- **Jig** — Device that locates a workpiece and guides a tool.
- **Chuck** — Workholding device that grips a workpiece or tool.
- **Collet** — Slotted clamping device used to hold a workpiece or tool.
- **Datum** — Reference feature or target used to establish dimensions or measurement.
- **Setup** — Configuration of machine, tooling, workholding, and process parameters.
- **Tool offset** — Stored adjustment accounting for tool position or geometry in CNC machining.
- **Work offset** — Coordinate offset defining a workpiece reference relative to the machine.
- **G-code** — Common family of numerical control commands for machine-tool motion and functions.
- **M-code** — Common family of auxiliary machine-control commands.
- **Postprocessor** — Software that converts manufacturing instructions into machine-specific code.
- **CAM (computer-aided manufacturing)** — Software and methods for planning or generating manufacturing operations.
- **Machining allowance** — Extra material reserved for a later finishing operation.
- **Burr** — Unwanted raised material left after cutting or machining.
- **Deburring** — Removing burrs and sharp edges.
- **Chamfer** — Beveled edge connecting two surfaces.
- **Fillet** — Rounded transition between surfaces or edges.
- **Surface finish** — Characteristics of surface texture, including roughness and waviness.
- **Surface roughness** — Fine-scale irregularity of a surface.
- **Ra** — Arithmetic average of the absolute surface-profile deviations from the mean line.
- **Rz** — Surface roughness parameter based on peak-to-valley measurements, with the exact definition depending on the standard.
- **Waviness** — Larger-scale surface undulations than typical roughness.
- **Flatness** — Geometric tolerance controlling how much a surface deviates from a perfect plane.
- **Roundness** — Geometric property controlling deviation from a perfect circle in a cross-section.
- **Cylindricity** — Geometric tolerance controlling the form of a cylindrical surface.
- **Runout** — Variation measured relative to a datum axis or reference as a part rotates.
- **Concentricity** — Relationship between centers or axes; in modern geometric tolerancing, more specific controls are often preferred.
- **Perpendicularity** — Orientation tolerance requiring a feature to be at 90 degrees to a datum.
- **Parallelism** — Orientation tolerance controlling parallel alignment relative to a datum.
- **Position tolerance** — Geometric control on the location of a feature.
- **Geometric dimensioning and tolerancing (GD&T)** — Standardized system for specifying allowable geometric variation.
- **Process capability** — Statistical ability of a process to meet specified requirements.
- **First-pass yield** — Fraction of units that meet requirements without rework.
- **Scrap rate** — Fraction of production rejected as unusable.
- **Rework** — Additional processing needed to bring a nonconforming part into compliance.
- **Lean manufacturing** — Approach focused on improving value and reducing waste.
- **Six Sigma** — Data-driven approach to reducing process variation and defects.
- **Kaizen** — Continuous improvement.
- **5S** — Workplace organization method: sort, set in order, shine, standardize, sustain.
- **Poka-yoke** — Error-proofing technique.
- **Kanban** — Visual or signal-based system for controlling workflow and replenishment.
- **Just-in-time (JIT)** — Production and supply approach intended to provide items when needed.
- **Overall equipment effectiveness (OEE)** — Composite measure of availability, performance, and quality.
- **Preventive maintenance** — Planned maintenance intended to reduce failures.
- **Predictive maintenance** — Maintenance based on condition data or forecasts.
- **Corrective maintenance** — Maintenance performed to address a detected fault or failure.
- **Root cause analysis** — Systematic process for identifying underlying causes of a problem.
- **Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA)** — Structured method for identifying possible failure modes and evaluating their effects.
- **Statistical process control (SPC)** — Use of statistical methods to monitor process behavior.
- **Control chart** — Plot used to distinguish process variation from signals that may indicate special causes.

## 11\. Welding, joining, and fabrication

- **Welding** — Joining materials through coalescence, with or without filler material.
- **Fusion welding** — Welding that melts the base material at the joint.
- **Solid-state welding** — Welding without melting the base materials.
- **Arc welding** — Welding using an electric arc as the heat source.
- **GMAW (gas metal arc welding)** — Arc welding using a continuously fed consumable wire and shielding gas; commonly called MIG welding.
- **GTAW (gas tungsten arc welding)** — Arc welding using a nonconsumable tungsten electrode; commonly called TIG welding.
- **SMAW (shielded metal arc welding)** — Arc welding using a flux-coated consumable electrode; commonly called stick welding.
- **FCAW (flux-cored arc welding)** — Arc welding using a tubular flux-filled wire.
- **SAW (submerged arc welding)** — Arc welding performed beneath a layer of granular flux.
- **Resistance welding** — Joining using electrical resistance heating and applied force.
- **Spot welding** — Resistance welding at localized points.
- **Seam welding** — Welding along a continuous seam, often using rotating electrodes.
- **Laser welding** — Joining using a focused laser heat source.
- **Electron beam welding** — Welding using a focused electron beam.
- **Friction welding** — Solid-state joining using friction-generated heat and pressure.
- **Friction stir welding** — Solid-state joining using a rotating tool to plasticize and mix material along a joint.
- **Brazing** — Joining using a filler metal that melts above 450°C but below the melting temperature of the base metals.
- **Soldering** — Joining using a filler metal that melts below 450°C.
- **Adhesive bonding** — Joining surfaces using an adhesive.
- **Riveting** — Permanent mechanical joining using rivets.
- **Press fit** — Joint made by forcing interference-fit components together.
- **Weld bead** — Deposited material formed by a welding pass.
- **Weld pool** — Localized molten metal during fusion welding.
- **Heat-affected zone (HAZ)** — Region whose properties or microstructure change due to welding heat.
- **Penetration** — Depth to which the weld extends into the joint.
- **Weld throat** — Effective cross-sectional dimension used to assess weld capacity.
- **Fillet weld** — Weld used to join surfaces meeting at an angle.
- **Groove weld** — Weld deposited in a groove between workpieces.
- **Butt joint** — Joint between components arranged approximately in the same plane.
- **Lap joint** — Joint in which components overlap.
- **Weld distortion** — Shape change caused by uneven heating and cooling.
- **Residual welding stress** — Stress remaining after welding and cooling.
- **Porosity** — Gas cavities trapped in weld metal.
- **Slag inclusion** — Nonmetallic material trapped in a weld.
- **Undercut** — Groove melted into the base material near the weld toe and left unfilled.
- **Lack of fusion** — Failure of weld metal to fuse adequately with the base material or preceding weld pass.
- **Lack of penetration** — Insufficient weld penetration into the joint.
- **Weld qualification** — Demonstration that a welding procedure or welder meets specified requirements.
- **Nondestructive testing (NDT)** — Evaluation without rendering the component unusable.
- **Visual testing (VT)** — Inspection by visual examination.
- **Dye penetrant testing (PT)** — Method for revealing surface-breaking discontinuities using penetrant.
- **Magnetic particle testing (MT)** — Method for detecting certain discontinuities in ferromagnetic materials.
- **Ultrasonic testing (UT)** — Inspection using high-frequency sound waves.
- **Radiographic testing (RT)** — Inspection using X-rays or gamma rays.
- **Destructive testing** — Testing that damages or destroys the specimen.
- **Tensile test** — Test measuring a material's response to tension.
- **Hardness test** — Test assessing resistance to indentation or penetration.
- **Charpy impact test** — Test measuring energy absorbed during impact fracture under specified conditions.

## 12\. CAD, engineering drawings, and design documentation

- **CAD (computer-aided design)** — Software-assisted creation and modification of engineering geometry.
- **2D drafting** — Creating two-dimensional technical drawings.
- **3D modeling** — Representing the geometry of three-dimensional objects.
- **Parametric modeling** — Modeling using dimensions, parameters, and relationships.
- **Direct modeling** — Editing geometry without relying primarily on a feature-history dependency tree.
- **Solid modeling** — Representation of a three-dimensional solid.
- **Surface modeling** — Representation of an object's surfaces without necessarily defining a solid volume.
- **Wireframe model** — Representation using points, curves, and edges.
- **Feature tree** — Ordered history of modeling operations and dependencies.
- **Sketch constraint** — Rule restricting sketch geometry.
- **Fully constrained sketch** — Sketch with no remaining unintended degrees of freedom.
- **Extrude** — Extending a profile along a direction to create geometry.
- **Revolve** — Creating geometry by rotating a profile about an axis.
- **Sweep** — Creating geometry by moving a profile along a path.
- **Loft** — Creating geometry through multiple cross-sections.
- **Fillet feature** — Adding a rounded transition to model edges or faces.
- **Chamfer feature** — Adding a beveled transition.
- **Pattern feature** — Repeating geometry in a defined arrangement.
- **Mirror feature** — Creating symmetric geometry about a plane or axis.
- **Boolean operation** — Combining or modifying solids through union, difference, or intersection.
- **Assembly** — Collection of components arranged to represent a product.
- **Mate** — CAD relationship restricting relative motion or position between components.
- **Exploded view** — Assembly representation with components separated for clarity.
- **Bill of materials (BOM)** — List of parts, quantities, and other procurement or manufacturing information.
- **Part number** — Identifier assigned to a specific component or configuration.
- **Revision** — Controlled version of an engineering document or design.
- **Design intent** — Rules and relationships governing how a model should behave when modified.
- **Reference geometry** — Points, planes, axes, or curves used to construct or locate features.
- **Datum plane** — Reference plane used for modeling, measurement, or manufacturing.
- **Coordinate frame** — Origin and axes defining a reference system.
- **Geometric constraint** — Relationship such as parallel, perpendicular, tangent, or coincident.
- **Dimensional constraint** — Numerical restriction on geometry.
- **Tolerance analysis** — Evaluation of dimensional variations and their effects on fit or function.
- **GD&T** — System of symbols controlling allowable variation in form, orientation, location, and related geometry.
- **Engineering drawing** — Technical document defining geometry, dimensions, tolerances, materials, and other requirements.
- **Orthographic projection** — Representation using views projected onto perpendicular planes.
- **Isometric view** — Pictorial view displaying three principal directions in a single representation.
- **Section view** — View showing internal features as if the object were cut.
- **Detail view** — Enlarged view of a selected area.
- **Auxiliary view** — View used to show a surface in its true shape or another useful orientation.
- **Hidden line** — Line representing an edge not directly visible in a view.
- **Centerline** — Line indicating an axis, center, or symmetry.
- **Dimension line** — Line indicating the extent and direction of a dimension.
- **Extension line** — Line connecting a feature to a dimension.
- **Leader line** — Line connecting an annotation to a feature.
- **Section hatching** — Pattern identifying material cut in a section view.
- **Title block** — Drawing area containing identification and administrative information.
- **General tolerance** — Default tolerance applying where no more specific tolerance is stated.
- **Fits and tolerances** — System for specifying acceptable dimensional variation between mating parts.
- **Datum reference frame** — Reference coordinate framework used for geometric tolerancing.
- **True position** — The theoretically exact location of a feature.
- **Profile tolerance** — Geometric control of a line or surface relative to a defined profile.
- **Flatness tolerance** — Control limiting a surface between two parallel planes.
- **Circularity tolerance** — Control limiting each circular cross-section to a specified zone.
- **Total runout** — Control of variation across an entire surface as the part rotates relative to a datum axis.
- **Interference detection** — Identifying overlapping solid volumes in an assembly.
- **Kinematic simulation** — Simulating mechanism motion and joint behavior.
- **Rendering** — Generating a visual representation using lighting, materials, and camera settings.
- **STEP file** — Neutral CAD exchange format widely used to transfer 3D geometry.
- **IGES file** — Neutral format for exchanging CAD geometry, particularly surfaces and curves.
- **STL file** — Triangulated surface format commonly used in 3D printing workflows.
- **DXF file** — Drawing exchange format commonly used for 2D geometry.
- **Native CAD file** — File format specific to a CAD application.
- **PDM (product data management)** — Management of engineering files, revisions, and product information.
- **PLM (product lifecycle management)** — Management of product information and processes throughout its lifecycle.
- **Digital twin** — Digital representation connected to a physical product or process through data and models.
- **Design for manufacturability (DFM)** — Designing parts so they can be manufactured effectively.
- **Design for assembly (DFA)** — Designing products to simplify and improve assembly.
- **Design for maintenance** — Designing for inspection, servicing, and repair.
- **Design for additive manufacturing (DfAM)** — Designing specifically for additive manufacturing capabilities and constraints.

## 13\. Finite element analysis and computational engineering

- **CAE (computer-aided engineering)** — Computer-based engineering analysis and simulation.
- **FEA (finite element analysis)** — Numerical method for approximating solutions to physical field problems.
- **Finite element method (FEM)** — Discretization-based numerical technique underlying many FEA tools.
- **Mesh** — Collection of elements and nodes used to discretize a model.
- **Node** — Point where degrees of freedom and solution variables are defined.
- **Element** — Discrete computational region connecting nodes.
- **Element type** — Formulation such as beam, shell, tetrahedral, or hexahedral.
- **Degree of freedom** — Independent variable associated with a node or system.
- **Boundary condition** — Prescribed displacement, force, temperature, or other condition.
- **Constraint** — Restriction on allowable motion or a model variable.
- **Load case** — Defined set of loads and boundary conditions.
- **Load combination** — Specified combination of loads for an analysis or design check.
- **Material model** — Mathematical description of a material's response.
- **Linear static analysis** — Analysis assuming linear behavior and static loading.
- **Nonlinear analysis** — Analysis incorporating relevant nonlinear material, geometric, or contact effects.
- **Geometric nonlinearity** — Nonlinearity associated with changes in geometry or deformation.
- **Material nonlinearity** — Nonlinear constitutive behavior such as plasticity.
- **Contact analysis** — Analysis of interacting surfaces.
- **Modal analysis** — Calculation of natural frequencies and mode shapes.
- **Harmonic response analysis** — Analysis of response to sinusoidal excitation.
- **Transient dynamic analysis** — Time-dependent analysis under changing loads.
- **Buckling analysis** — Evaluation of structural instability.
- **Thermal analysis** — Calculation of temperature distributions and thermal responses.
- **Thermomechanical analysis** — Coupled or sequential analysis of thermal and mechanical effects.
- **Computational fluid dynamics (CFD)** — Numerical simulation of fluid flow and related physical phenomena.
- **Computational domain** — Region represented in a numerical simulation.
- **Grid independence** — Demonstration that key results are sufficiently insensitive to further mesh refinement.
- **Mesh convergence** — Process of checking solution stability as the mesh is refined.
- **Time-step independence** — Checking whether a transient solution is sufficiently insensitive to time-step refinement.
- **Solver** — Algorithm that calculates a numerical solution.
- **Direct solver** — Solver that uses direct algebraic methods to solve a system.
- **Iterative solver** — Solver that approaches a solution through repeated updates.
- **Convergence criterion** — Condition used to decide whether a numerical solution has converged.
- **Residual** — Measure of how closely a computed solution satisfies the governing equations.
- **Numerical stability** — Resistance of a computational method to uncontrolled growth of numerical errors.
- **Numerical diffusion** — Artificial smoothing of a solution caused by discretization.
- **Stress contour** — Visual representation of stress distribution.
- **Displacement contour** — Visual representation of displacement magnitude or components.
- **Factor of safety plot** — Display of calculated design margins under specified criteria.
- **Singularity** — Location where an idealized mathematical model predicts unbounded or mesh-sensitive results.
- **Verification** — Checking that the equations are solved correctly.
- **Validation** — Checking whether the model adequately represents physical reality.
- **Model correlation** — Comparing model predictions with measurements or test results.
- **Sensitivity study** — Examining how modeling assumptions or inputs influence results.
- **Reduced-order model** — Simplified model designed to capture important behavior at lower computational cost.
- **Multiphysics simulation** — Simulation involving interacting physical fields such as fluid flow, heat, and structural deformation.

## 14\. Vibrations, dynamics, and noise

- **Free vibration** — Oscillation following an initial disturbance without sustained external excitation.
- **Forced vibration** — Oscillation driven by external excitation.
- **Natural frequency** — Frequency at which a system tends to oscillate in a free-vibration mode.
- **Fundamental frequency** — Lowest natural frequency of a specified system or mode family.
- **Mode shape** — Spatial deformation pattern associated with a natural frequency.
- **Resonance** — Large response when excitation interacts strongly with a system's natural dynamics.
- **Damping ratio** — Ratio of actual damping to critical damping in a standard second-order model.
- **Critical damping** — Damping level separating oscillatory from nonoscillatory responses in a simple second-order system.
- **Underdamped system** — System that oscillates while its response decays.
- **Overdamped system** — System that returns without oscillation in the standard second-order model.
- **Critically damped system** — System at the boundary between underdamped and overdamped behavior.
- **Logarithmic decrement** — Measure of the decay in successive vibration amplitudes.
- **Amplitude** — Magnitude of an oscillating quantity.
- **Frequency** — Number of cycles per unit time.
- **Period** — Time required for one cycle.
- **Phase** — Relative position within an oscillation cycle.
- **Phase angle** — Angular measure of phase difference.
- **Harmonic motion** — Motion represented by sinusoidal functions.
- **Simple harmonic motion (SHM)** — Ideal oscillation with restoring acceleration proportional to displacement and directed toward equilibrium.
- **Forced-response function** — Mathematical description of a system's response to a specified input.
- **Frequency response** — Relationship between sinusoidal input and output as excitation frequency changes.
- **Transfer function** — Input-output representation of a linear time-invariant system in the Laplace domain.
- **Frequency response function (FRF)** — Frequency-dependent relationship between measured or modeled input and output.
- **Fourier transform** — Mathematical operation decomposing a signal into frequency components.
- **Fast Fourier transform (FFT)** — Efficient algorithm for computing a discrete Fourier transform.
- **Power spectral density (PSD)** — Distribution of signal power across frequency.
- **Vibration spectrum** — Representation of vibration amplitude or power against frequency.
- **RMS vibration** — Root-mean-square measure of vibration magnitude.
- **Peak-to-peak amplitude** — Difference between maximum and minimum values.
- **Unbalance** — Uneven mass distribution in a rotating assembly.
- **Static balancing** — Balancing intended to correct the resultant centrifugal force.
- **Dynamic balancing** — Balancing that addresses both resultant force and couple effects.
- **Critical speed** — Rotational speed near a relevant system natural frequency or instability condition.
- **Whirling** — Lateral motion of a rotating shaft.
- **Torsional vibration** — Oscillation in angular displacement or torque.
- **Misalignment** — Deviation from intended alignment of connected rotating components.
- **Mechanical looseness** — Unwanted movement due to excessive clearance or insufficient fastening.
- **Gear mesh frequency** — Frequency at which gear teeth engage.
- **Bearing defect frequency** — Characteristic frequency associated with certain rolling-element bearing defects.
- **Acoustic noise** — Unwanted or measured sound.
- **Sound pressure level (SPL)** — Logarithmic measure of sound pressure relative to a reference.
- **Decibel (dB)** — Logarithmic unit for expressing ratios of power, intensity, or amplitude under defined conventions.
- **Sound intensity** — Acoustic power transmitted per unit area.
- **Acoustic resonance** — Strong acoustic response near a resonant frequency.
- **Modal testing** — Experimental identification of modal properties.
- **Operational deflection shape (ODS)** — Observed vibration shape under actual operating excitation.
- **Vibration isolation** — Reducing vibration transmission between a source and a receiver.
- **Transmissibility** — Ratio of transmitted response amplitude to input amplitude under specified conditions.
- **Shock loading** — Rapid loading that produces a transient response.
- **Impact response** — System response to a short-duration impact.
- **Condition monitoring** — Tracking equipment health through measurements such as vibration, temperature, or oil condition.

## 15\. Control systems and automation

- **Control system** — System that regulates the behavior of a process or machine.
- **Open-loop control** — Control without measuring the output to correct the input.
- **Closed-loop control** — Control that uses feedback from the system output.
- **Feedback** — Information about system output used to influence its behavior.
- **Setpoint** — Desired value of a controlled variable.
- **Process variable (PV)** — Measured or estimated value of the variable being controlled.
- **Manipulated variable (MV)** — Input adjusted by a controller.
- **Disturbance** — Unwanted or external influence on a system.
- **Error signal** — Difference between the setpoint and the measured value, using the chosen sign convention.
- **Controller** — Device or algorithm that determines control actions.
- **Plant** — Process or physical system being controlled.
- **Sensor** — Device that measures a physical quantity.
- **Transducer** — Device that converts one form of energy or a physical quantity into another signal.
- **Actuator** — Device that converts a command and energy input into physical action.
- **PID controller** — Controller combining proportional, integral, and derivative actions.
- **Proportional control** — Control action proportional to current error.
- **Integral control** — Control action based on accumulated error.
- **Derivative control** — Control action related to the rate of change of error.
- **PID tuning** — Selecting controller parameters to achieve desired performance.
- **Overshoot** — Amount by which a response exceeds its target.
- **Undershoot** — Amount by which a response falls below its target.
- **Rise time** — Time for a response to move through a specified fraction of its final change.
- **Settling time** — Time required for a response to remain within a specified band around its final value.
- **Steady-state error** — Difference between desired and actual steady-state output.
- **Stability** — Tendency of a system's response to remain bounded or return toward an operating condition, under the relevant definition.
- **Robustness** — Ability to maintain acceptable performance despite uncertainty or disturbances.
- **Gain** — Ratio or sensitivity relating input and output.
- **Phase margin** — Frequency-domain measure of relative stability for a feedback system.
- **Gain margin** — Frequency-domain measure of how much loop gain can increase before reaching a stability boundary.
- **Bode plot** — Frequency-response plots of magnitude and phase against frequency.
- **Root locus** — Plot showing how closed-loop poles move as a system parameter changes.
- **Pole** — Value of the complex variable where a transfer function's denominator becomes zero, after applicable cancellations.
- **Zero** — Value of the complex variable where a transfer function's numerator becomes zero.
- **State-space model** — Representation using state variables and first-order differential equations.
- **Transfer function** — Input-output model for a linear time-invariant system under specified conditions.
- **Linear time-invariant (LTI) system** — System whose behavior is linear and whose parameters do not vary with time.
- **Discrete-time control** — Control implemented at sampled time instants.
- **Sampling rate** — Number of samples taken per unit time.
- **Sampling period** — Time between successive samples.
- **Quantization** — Mapping a continuous range of values to discrete levels.
- **PLC (programmable logic controller)** — Industrial controller designed for automation tasks.
- **Ladder logic** — Graphical PLC programming language resembling relay circuits.
- **HMI (human-machine interface)** — Interface through which an operator monitors or controls equipment.
- **SCADA** — Supervisory control and data acquisition system.
- **Industrial automation** — Use of control, sensing, and machinery to operate industrial processes.
- **Interlock** — Logic or hardware preventing an action unless specified conditions are met.
- **Limit switch** — Switch activated by reaching a particular position or condition.
- **Proximity sensor** — Sensor that detects an object's presence or nearness without necessarily making contact.
- **Encoder** — Device providing position, speed, or motion-related feedback.
- **Resolver** — Electromechanical sensor used to determine angular position.
- **Servo motor** — Motor used in a feedback-controlled motion system.
- **Stepper motor** — Motor designed to move in discrete angular increments.
- **VFD (variable-frequency drive)** — Device controlling AC motor speed by varying supply frequency and related electrical parameters.
- **Soft starter** — Device that limits motor starting current or torque by controlling the starting process.
- **Solenoid** — Electromagnetic actuator that commonly produces linear movement.
- **Electropneumatic control** — Pneumatic operation controlled using electrical signals and components.
- **Electrohydraulic control** — Hydraulic operation regulated by electrical control elements.
- **Robotics** — Study and application of programmable mechanical systems.
- **Degrees of freedom in robotics** — Independent joint coordinates describing a robot's configuration.
- **Forward kinematics** — Calculating end-effector pose from joint coordinates.
- **Inverse kinematics** — Finding joint coordinates needed to achieve a desired end-effector pose.
- **Workspace** — Set of positions or poses a robot can reach.
- **End effector** — Tool or device attached to the end of a robot arm.
- **Trajectory planning** — Defining a path and its timing for motion.
- **Path planning** — Determining a feasible route between configurations or locations.
- **Machine vision** — Using imaging and computational analysis for inspection, identification, or guidance.
- **Force control** — Controlling applied force rather than position alone.
- **Compliant mechanism** — Mechanism obtaining motion through elastic deformation of flexible members.

## 16\. Electrical engineering for mechanical engineers

- **Voltage** — Electric potential difference.
- **Current** — Rate of electric charge flow.
- **Resistance** — Opposition to electric current.
- **Ohm's law** — Relationship $V=IR$ for an ohmic element under specified conditions.
- **Electrical power** — Rate of electrical energy transfer.
- **Electrical energy** — Energy transferred by an electrical system.
- **Direct current (DC)** — Current flowing in one direction.
- **Alternating current (AC)** — Current that varies periodically and reverses direction in common AC systems.
- **Frequency** — Number of electrical cycles per second.
- **Phase** — Relative position within a periodic waveform.
- **Single-phase power** — AC power supplied using a single phase.
- **Three-phase power** — AC power supplied through three phase-displaced waveforms.
- **Power factor** — Ratio of real power to apparent power.
- **Real power** — Average power transferred to perform useful work or dissipate energy.
- **Reactive power** — AC power oscillating between sources and reactive elements.
- **Apparent power** — Product of RMS voltage and RMS current in the standard single-phase definition.
- **Transformer** — Device that transfers electrical energy between circuits through electromagnetic induction.
- **Circuit breaker** — Protective device that interrupts current under specified fault conditions.
- **Fuse** — Protective device that opens a circuit when excessive current melts its element.
- **Relay** — Electrically operated switch.
- **Contactor** — Switching device designed for frequent operation of power circuits.
- **Grounding** — Connection to earth or a defined reference ground, depending on the system.
- **Insulation** — Material or system restricting unwanted electrical current flow.
- **Short circuit** — Unintended low-impedance connection between points at different potentials.
- **Overload** — Operating condition exceeding a component's rated load.
- **Electric motor** — Machine converting electrical energy into mechanical energy.
- **Generator** — Machine converting mechanical energy into electrical energy.
- **Electromagnetic induction** — Generation of electromotive force by changing magnetic flux linkage.
- **Back electromotive force (back EMF)** — Voltage generated by a motor's motion that opposes the applied voltage under the usual motor convention.
- **Torque constant** — Relationship between motor current and electromagnetic torque under a specified convention.
- **Motor efficiency** — Mechanical output power divided by electrical input power.
- **Induction motor** — AC motor in which rotor current is induced by the stator's magnetic field.
- **Synchronous motor** — AC motor whose steady-state rotor speed matches the rotating magnetic field's synchronous speed.
- **Servo drive** — Electronic drive that controls a motor, often using feedback.
- **Power electronics** — Electronic conversion and control of electrical power.
- **Rectifier** — Device converting AC to DC.
- **Inverter** — Device converting DC to AC.
- **DC-DC converter** — Device converting one DC voltage level to another.
- **Battery management system (BMS)** — System monitoring and controlling battery operation and protection.
- **Thermistor** — Resistor whose resistance changes substantially with temperature.
- **RTD (resistance temperature detector)** — Temperature sensor using a calibrated resistance-temperature relationship.
- **Thermocouple** — Temperature sensor based on thermoelectric voltage.
- **Strain gauge** — Sensor whose electrical resistance changes with strain.
- **Load cell** — Transducer that measures force or weight.
- **LVDT (linear variable differential transformer)** — Electromechanical sensor for measuring linear displacement.
- **Hall-effect sensor** — Sensor that uses the Hall effect to detect magnetic fields or related quantities.
- **Accelerometer** — Sensor measuring acceleration.
- **Gyroscope** — Sensor measuring angular velocity or orientation-related quantities, depending on its design.
- **Data acquisition (DAQ)** — Collection of sensor signals through electronic measurement systems.
- **Signal conditioning** — Amplifying, filtering, isolating, or otherwise preparing signals for measurement or control.
- **Analog signal** — Signal represented by a continuously varying quantity.
- **Digital signal** — Signal represented using discrete values.
- **Low-pass filter** — Filter that attenuates frequencies above a selected range.
- **High-pass filter** — Filter that attenuates frequencies below a selected range.
- **Bandwidth** — Frequency range over which a system or component meets a specified response criterion.
- **Electromagnetic compatibility (EMC)** — Ability of equipment to operate without unacceptable electromagnetic interference.
- **Electromagnetic interference (EMI)** — Unwanted electromagnetic disturbance affecting equipment.
- **Electrical schematic** — Diagram showing electrical connections and functional relationships.

## 17\. Internal combustion engines and powertrains

- **Internal combustion engine (ICE)** — Engine in which combustion occurs within the working chamber.
- **Spark-ignition engine (SI)** — Engine that initiates combustion using a spark.
- **Compression-ignition engine (CI)** — Engine that initiates combustion through compression heating, typically with fuel injection.
- **Four-stroke engine** — Engine operating through intake, compression, power, and exhaust strokes.
- **Two-stroke engine** — Engine completing its cycle in two piston strokes.
- **Cylinder** — Chamber in which a piston moves.
- **Piston** — Reciprocating component that transmits gas forces to a connecting rod.
- **Piston ring** — Ring that helps seal the combustion chamber and control oil.
- **Connecting rod** — Link between piston and crankshaft.
- **Crankshaft** — Shaft converting reciprocating motion into rotation or vice versa.
- **Crankcase** — Structure housing or supporting the crankshaft and related components.
- **Camshaft** — Shaft with cams that control valve or other follower motion.
- **Intake valve** — Valve controlling entry of fresh charge into the cylinder.
- **Exhaust valve** — Valve controlling discharge of exhaust gases.
- **Valve timing** — Timing of valve opening and closing relative to crankshaft position.
- **Valve overlap** — Period during which intake and exhaust valves are simultaneously open.
- **Ignition timing** — Timing of spark initiation relative to engine position.
- **Fuel injection** — Metered delivery of fuel into an intake or combustion region.
- **Carburetor** — Device that meters and mixes fuel with air in certain engines.
- **Turbocharger** — Compressor driven by an exhaust-gas turbine.
- **Supercharger** — Mechanically or electrically driven intake-air compressor.
- **Intercooler** — Heat exchanger that cools compressed intake air between compression stages or before engine entry.
- **Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR)** — Returning a controlled portion of exhaust gas to the intake to influence combustion and emissions.
- **Air-fuel ratio (AFR)** — Ratio of air mass to fuel mass.
- **Lambda (****$\lambda$****)** — Actual air-fuel ratio divided by the stoichiometric air-fuel ratio.
- **Volumetric efficiency** — Measure comparing actual inducted air volume or mass with an appropriate theoretical reference.
- **Compression ratio** — Ratio of maximum cylinder volume to minimum cylinder volume.
- **Displacement volume** — Volume swept by the piston between top and bottom dead center.
- **Top dead center (TDC)** — Piston position nearest the cylinder head.
- **Bottom dead center (BDC)** — Piston position farthest from the cylinder head.
- **Mean effective pressure (MEP)** — Hypothetical constant pressure that would produce the same net work over a cycle.
- **Indicated power** — Power developed by gas forces inside the cylinders.
- **Brake power** — Useful power measured at the engine output shaft.
- **Friction power** — Difference between indicated power and brake power under consistent measurement conditions.
- **Brake-specific fuel consumption (BSFC)** — Fuel mass consumed per unit brake power per unit time.
- **Thermal efficiency** — Useful engine work divided by the fuel energy input, using a defined basis.
- **Mechanical efficiency** — Brake power divided by indicated power under consistent definitions.
- **Knock** — Abnormal combustion involving rapid pressure oscillations, with mechanisms depending on engine type.
- **Pre-ignition** — Ignition of the charge before the intended spark in an SI engine.
- **Detonation** — Rapid combustion or shock-related process; its exact meaning depends on the context.
- **Octane rating** — Measure of a gasoline's resistance to knock under standardized test conditions.
- **Cetane number** — Measure related to ignition quality of diesel fuel.
- **Diesel particulate filter (DPF)** — Device that traps particulate matter from diesel exhaust.
- **Catalytic converter** — Exhaust-treatment device that catalyzes chemical reactions to reduce emissions.
- **Selective catalytic reduction (SCR)** — Emissions-control process that uses a reductant to reduce nitrogen oxides.
- **Exhaust gas temperature (EGT)** — Temperature of engine exhaust gas at a specified location.
- **Dynamometer** — Device measuring torque and rotational speed to determine power.
- **Engine map** — Representation of engine performance over operating conditions such as speed and load.
- **Transmission** — Mechanism changing speed and torque between a power source and a driven system.
- **Manual transmission** — Transmission with gear selection controlled by the operator.
- **Automatic transmission** — Transmission that selects ratios automatically.
- **Continuously variable transmission (CVT)** — Transmission providing a continuous range of ratios within its operating limits.
- **Differential** — Gear mechanism allowing connected outputs to rotate at different speeds.
- **Final drive** — Gear reduction transmitting power to the driven wheels or output.
- **Driveshaft** — Shaft transmitting rotational power.
- **Half-shaft** — Shaft connecting a differential or transmission output to a driven wheel.
- **Clutch engagement** — Process of establishing torque transmission across a clutch.
- **Gear shifting** — Changing the transmission ratio.
- **Torque converter** — Fluid coupling device that can multiply torque under certain conditions.
- **Regenerative braking** — Recovering kinetic energy and converting it into stored or reusable energy.
- **Hybrid powertrain** — Powertrain combining multiple propulsion energy sources, commonly an engine and electric motor.
- **Electric vehicle (EV)** — Vehicle propelled partly or entirely by electric motors.
- **Battery electric vehicle (BEV)** — Vehicle propelled by electricity stored in a rechargeable battery.
- **Hybrid electric vehicle (HEV)** — Vehicle combining an internal combustion engine and electric propulsion.
- **Plug-in hybrid electric vehicle (PHEV)** — Hybrid vehicle with an externally rechargeable traction battery.

